    "case_id": "586",
    "case_summary": "A 29-year-old woman from the Dominican Republic, who moved to the United States in 2014, presented in January 2016 for prenatal care. A purified protein derivative skin test was positive (21 mm). In January 2022, she was started on rifampin (300 mg daily) for a planned 4-month course. She was taking no other medications. After three months of therapy, she experienced two days of nausea, vomiting, and subjective fevers.\n\nInitial presentation showed no significant physical examination findings noted in the case report.\n\n{'Laboratory Tests': ['- Baseline (January 2022):', '  * Creatinine: 0.96 mg/dL', '  * Normal liver function tests and blood counts', '- At Emergency Department presentation (April 2022):', '  * Creatinine: 3.8 mg/dL', '  * Blood urea nitrogen: 41 mg/dL', '  * Platelets: 84 K/μL', '  * Aspartate aminotransferase: 115 U/L', '  * Hemoglobin: 12.1 g/dL', '  * Haptoglobin: <10 mg/dL', '  * Lactate dehydrogenase: 1125 U/L', '  * Urine protein: 102.7 mg/dL', '  * Mild hematuria'], 'Imaging Studies': ['- Chest radiograph (2016): showed no abnormalities', '- Renal ultrasound: markedly echogenic renal cortices bilaterally, consistent with parenchymal disease but no hydronephrosis'], 'Pathology': {'Renal Biopsy': {'Title': 'Figure 1. Renal Biopsy Findings', 'Description': ['A) Many tubules in the cortex contain eosinophilic proteinaceous material and cell debris (hematoxylin–eosin stain, 20× objective)', 'B) The content of these tubules shows strong reactivity for hemoglobin by immunohistochemistry (immunoperoxidase stain, 20× objective)']}}}",
